[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256480 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "568e2b05-f879-518d-a7b1-158ba643f588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254432} {:answer/id #uuid "6206ecb7-b831-5615-8736-80afa9e26e1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254550} {:answer/id #uuid "6658071b-824b-50e8-8627-8d323ef0f717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254590} {:answer/id #uuid "a24fb539-94a1-50c1-94ac-c8fcce9c9127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3e}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255102} {:answer/id #uuid "d9b297a7-67f8-59a5-a6fd-631cbd108555" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255601}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6658071b-824b-50e8-8627-8d323ef0f717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254590} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc5aa464-e530-5e28-8713-7daa3102361f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5d0b02b3-02ea-5d58-9f01-5a623c01d6e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-120842" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}{e}^{x},\\,y(0)=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=1?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $$ | $$ | $$ | $\\,?\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={y}^{2}{e}^{x} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot e \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{16} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{16}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{e}{16}$ | $\\frac{e}{16}$ |"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256483 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d9f493f-2f7f-597c-b1cd-e62f3ee09834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{121}^{∘},-{59}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254203} {:answer/id #uuid "8854e260-1cb4-52ad-a0ba-7c92f153540b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{107}^{∘},-{73}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254880} {:answer/id #uuid "8d3b0e1b-f60a-5382-8a40-c5eee04082e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{111}^{∘},-{69}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254916} {:answer/id #uuid "a915b31b-6b2c-5c88-b7d7-7f5b719af6cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{104}^{∘},-{76}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255161} {:answer/id #uuid "f5ce1226-4a3f-5fab-81dc-1cba3d7a68a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{117}^{∘},-{63}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5ce1226-4a3f-5fab-81dc-1cba3d7a68a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{117}^{∘},-{63}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255821} :answer-field/id #uuid "2197222f-f438-5f03-9f99-4069fd3f9dd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c4999664-f9f8-55c5-b29e-56d4b2fd62ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-120856" :question/problem "Calculate, in degrees, rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions to the equation $9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-8$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-8 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-\\frac{8}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{8}{9})=-62.733\\,955{…}^{∘}≈-{62.73}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-{62.73}^{∘}$ lies inside the range, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-{62.73}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={62.73}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={62.73}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/62/62808ce876d31757e1b46863b014bbf83209c414099ade0f93b91303c6702d1f.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{62.73}^{∘} \\\\  & ={242.73}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution ${x}_{2}$ that's within the given domain, we simply subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=X-{360}^{∘}={242.73}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{117.27}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions (rounded to the nearest integer) are $x=-{117}^{∘},-{63}^{∘}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256486 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "418f6787-986a-5387-a823-d34973529cf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254228} {:answer/id #uuid "4f21de89-5b0b-57fe-92f9-99c9f6fc1ca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254356} {:answer/id #uuid "6e4a5356-7c2c-5ab9-b12a-a020decffb54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254649} {:answer/id #uuid "a62b88b8-7a69-5e4d-9e20-98d279c7c1a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255132} {:answer/id #uuid "d849473e-b8e1-531d-86cb-6519efb6857b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e4a5356-7c2c-5ab9-b12a-a020decffb54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254649} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1672532-d882-5ce8-b2ba-6c9ab6442494" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4bbca5fa-199a-5992-9918-2001be7e80c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-120887" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $4\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2\\sqrt{5}=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2\\sqrt{5} & =0 \\\\ 4\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})={60}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${60}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={60}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/9c/9c8adbb0a1594ae5bacfab7f1f92993c67659839599e10ab46d5cf98896e3ec7.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={300}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x={60}^{∘},\\,{300}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is $60\\cdot 300=18\\,000.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256487 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e6148f0-c0ef-5ec8-84fa-96f7399895bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254084} {:answer/id #uuid "aa374c04-93de-5b00-a6c3-ee0239b5dbe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255176} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ec00b1-8b3d-578e-aa9d-b52b625b0b7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255422} {:answer/id #uuid "e0199b8f-172f-517c-8832-b2230b1dbb50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255662} {:answer/id #uuid "f9df6008-3042-5fe7-bfd0-7945f4fd4d90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0199b8f-172f-517c-8832-b2230b1dbb50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255662} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8ca042d-c28f-56ba-b846-2e285284fac0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256252}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "814abbe2-963b-55aa-99fd-84de01c3f66e" :question/math-academy-id "q-120893" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{7}{8}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{7}{8})=0.505\\,360…≈0.505\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0.505\\,4$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=0.505\\,4.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.505\\,4.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.505\\,4.$\n\n![](images/49/4979d3f7031e70a642e6995c3eb819d2238281c1312a3a32c2eb4fe8dd576c24.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -0.505\\,4 \\\\  & ≈5.777\\,8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=0.505\\,4\\cdot 5.777\\,8≈2.9\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256488 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51288993-1186-54f7-b8f9-c3dedec828cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254373} {:answer/id #uuid "6636f841-f6b2-5a4a-8b18-128444b21885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254587} {:answer/id #uuid "a7ed8e9d-6c96-5472-ab47-f9b913bb9f05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255152} {:answer/id #uuid "a96a54c4-bfd5-505c-91fe-009524c103c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255163} {:answer/id #uuid "eac5a2ac-906b-5eeb-80d9-346addcba2be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eac5a2ac-906b-5eeb-80d9-346addcba2be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255725} :answer-field/id #uuid "b53684b1-60b5-56d0-bc46-63f1c5c26e72" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "70af0da8-cc0a-5a44-abbf-395198d56bd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-120896" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{7}{9}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{7}{9}) & =2.461\\,918…≈2.461\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2.461\\,9$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=2.461\\,9.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -2.461\\,9 & ≈0.679\\,7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.679\\,7.$\n\n![](images/ea/eafc9be7094c9c0ccdf167cc737d494efec40014a89ad793f7ed34ea5eed94ae.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.679\\,7 \\\\  & ≈3.821\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2.461\\,9\\cdot 3.821\\,3≈9\n$$\n\nto the nearest integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256495 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a1b4c3b-0872-5484-846b-7c31f075326b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253748} {:answer/id #uuid "5d66023e-f256-5b13-916d-42dcaa6c013c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254497} {:answer/id #uuid "791eb4cd-5e49-57d9-b7e6-a52a40c5e041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254743} {:answer/id #uuid "7ff67ebc-3568-54d4-9dbc-25c3ce24692f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254803} {:answer/id #uuid "be503456-5109-5ce6-90f6-46f497a9e83d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255368}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ff67ebc-3568-54d4-9dbc-25c3ce24692f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254803} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee3993dd-3a89-5d8f-8088-b70e6d9fbfde" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1acd4d45-a455-5e34-97cf-5720edb8c513" :question/math-academy-id "q-120981" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{9}{11}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{9}{11})=0.612\\,554…≈0.612\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0.612\\,6$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=0.612\\,6.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.612\\,6.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.612\\,6.$\n\n![](images/23/23712114dae7013de5786e51b99cbd0857c41ed6443e0cb6a9f54aa4ba00dcbb.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -0.612\\,6 \\\\  & ≈5.670\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=0.612\\,6\\cdot 5.670\\,6≈3.5\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256499 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24314c99-31d8-5880-aa80-859ddebf537a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,025" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253995} {:answer/id #uuid "2c6048fe-e5d4-58cd-b52e-8d7b775d71f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\,050" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254062} {:answer/id #uuid "5a50e5ba-6ec8-5e4d-9342-dfd97b243043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,050" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254464} {:answer/id #uuid "bf757129-24df-5e8e-88a8-4c241ddb01c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\,050" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255376} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b974b9-30c6-5ccc-992a-bbc09cb083c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24314c99-31d8-5880-aa80-859ddebf537a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,025" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253995} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ec9988b-0e13-5100-b48c-cd52948a6cdd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1a5320c3-6f32-5efe-979e-244ce14b368d" :question/math-academy-id "q-120997" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3\\sqrt{2}=0$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3\\sqrt{2} & =0 \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${45}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={45}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/c7/c722d4fa2b77011e4df5b086c19bb09943446941484cfaf87559ca39a52183bc.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract ${360}^{∘}$ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{45}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\pm {45}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=45\\cdot (-45)=-2\\,025.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256502 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16d5d73a-c6b8-5a51-beb9-cf9785547807" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253867} {:answer/id #uuid "2088b354-bba0-5bbd-92cd-c954b048e3d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253953} {:answer/id #uuid "9953dc23-1bd9-5f0e-a559-57fc3038241d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14\\,400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255024} {:answer/id #uuid "b5b86b8d-2ed5-562e-9f4b-4def339bac52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12\\,400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255275} {:answer/id #uuid "fe653fa6-b37c-55d8-9125-0cdf8cffe696" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10\\,400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9953dc23-1bd9-5f0e-a559-57fc3038241d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14\\,400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255024} :answer-field/id #uuid "13eb25ff-2c80-5b31-9e6d-23396a052f5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "879c6f82-eae3-56cf-9655-29a95317031b" :question/math-academy-id "q-121001" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1=\\frac{3}{4}$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1 & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})={120}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${120}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={120}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/ec/ec1135cb0a4d35130a1c11baf9f421a3cdb4bc22cc1b436686dce722fa3c812e.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={240}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract ${360}^{∘}$ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & ={240}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{120}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\pm {120}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=120\\cdot (-120)=-14\\,400.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256504 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cf84eac-dfb9-56b6-9d35-09cf8be3a8db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254069} {:answer/id #uuid "79fa5683-dcc5-5621-894a-23286b3ba212" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254750} {:answer/id #uuid "ab0d34c0-b01b-5310-8159-7529d4e57357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255184} {:answer/id #uuid "c6022b9e-4816-5d00-b707-a5a19bbeb315" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255441} {:answer/id #uuid "de8035dc-1598-5061-9ed6-07af3f5e33ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2cf84eac-dfb9-56b6-9d35-09cf8be3a8db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254069} :answer-field/id #uuid "c06c3b43-7e9a-599a-8450-3f6eb8c9b5ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e663c698-6d0d-52a5-9b2e-c44b567d2f32" :question/math-academy-id "q-121007" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t\\ge 0$ is given by $v(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ At time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ the particle is located at the position $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=\\pi ?$" :question/worked-solution "The position of the particle at $t=\\pi $ is equal to its position at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\pi ].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(\\pi )=x(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{2}+{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+{[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})]}_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+([-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3})]-[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{3})]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-(-\\frac{1}{2})+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=\\pi $ is $x=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256509 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22923d7e-c2bf-54db-9229-af891faaaba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253981} {:answer/id #uuid "42fc831c-f373-5a08-b7fb-1e79b4be82b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254243} {:answer/id #uuid "5b621601-0d36-5470-8709-c19fa44a6277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254478} {:answer/id #uuid "5d5faadb-3fdd-57c3-b2c4-ec15ab10ec8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254496} {:answer/id #uuid "8ce3dc42-d8bc-552a-9152-ace8a49e8b52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b621601-0d36-5470-8709-c19fa44a6277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254478} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebec49e5-7b30-5233-8ff9-e5bb67a9a371" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48b98ebe-1777-506b-aeab-b7357f560c5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-121063" :question/problem "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx={a}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Which of the following could be the value of $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2} & ={30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the above equation for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\cdot {30}^{∘}+2\\cdot n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $a={60}^{∘}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256512 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fc83d8d-3136-580b-8b19-32178afb3100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "360" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254220} {:answer/id #uuid "53b4e98d-7eb5-5735-a881-183a34833cad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "240" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254399} {:answer/id #uuid "5a8d4f78-eaec-535a-b25e-e0823220e31b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254468} {:answer/id #uuid "a425d0c2-c38a-59d2-b8b5-610f1e79c890" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "270" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255114} {:answer/id #uuid "f472452c-4bed-5359-816c-f5660a061ece" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53b4e98d-7eb5-5735-a881-183a34833cad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "240" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254399} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b93f2ab-b88a-50a6-84c5-961f0ef41a89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "29983882-90b9-5637-81ac-662f4d53cf33" :question/math-academy-id "q-121068" :question/problem "Consider the following trigonometric equation:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})=-2.\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation is\n\n$$\nx={a}^{∘}+{720}^{∘}\\cdot n,\\,x={480}^{∘}+{720}^{∘}\\cdot n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Which of the following could be the value of $a?$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}={120}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n,\\,\\frac{x}{2}={240}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n.\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2} & ={120}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n, & \\,\\frac{x}{2} & ={240}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ x & ={240}^{∘}+{720}^{∘}\\cdot n, & \\,x & ={480}^{∘}+{720}^{∘}\\cdot n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have that $a=240.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256513 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19df14ba-5dff-52bc-9940-bab3de1d3682" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "450" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253889} {:answer/id #uuid "5d8173b5-0b39-5a85-9669-251abaf22a6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "360" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254498} {:answer/id #uuid "a43acbfb-eada-51d0-b0a1-fef515247743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "240" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255116} {:answer/id #uuid "a462c40e-07c9-5814-86af-f4b31004d1fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "270" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255119} {:answer/id #uuid "c580f8ac-35a5-51ee-8d60-de7839858bef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "480" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d8173b5-0b39-5a85-9669-251abaf22a6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "360" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254498} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f690fcf-ed13-5307-b662-ff8a8ce78405" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8b568634-0b04-5e19-bef7-4effff3320c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-121071" :question/problem "Consider the following trigonometric  equation:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx={180}^{∘}+n\\cdot 1\\,{080}^{∘},\\,x={a}^{∘}+n\\cdot 1\\,{080}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Which of the following could be the value of $a?$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{3}={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,\\frac{x}{3}={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\nx={180}^{∘}+n\\cdot 1\\,{080}^{∘},\\,x={360}^{∘}+n\\cdot 1\\,{080}^{∘}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nSo, we have that $a=360.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256517 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e3f6f5f-613d-5f09-8751-7270a136d06e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253792} {:answer/id #uuid "36114dc8-432d-5cd5-ba5d-2485549449ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254136} {:answer/id #uuid "5a44aa00-0642-5dd1-98a9-4773a3e72b01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254463} {:answer/id #uuid "dc904c1b-49ea-5c72-afec-46c46aae2dac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255625} {:answer/id #uuid "df0a2d64-ea89-55d6-b761-dadc8b523e4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255649}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df0a2d64-ea89-55d6-b761-dadc8b523e4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255649} :answer-field/id #uuid "86c68af2-ad02-5990-b605-08482b42c1df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256172}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "84fb9695-2616-55d3-bd8c-4022cb09276f" :question/math-academy-id "q-121119" :question/problem "Solve the equation $7\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+2\\sqrt{3}=-5\\sqrt{3}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+2\\sqrt{3} & =-5\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 7\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-7\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\pi +x=\\pi +(-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =2\\pi +x=2\\pi +(-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256538 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2365fdbc-0af4-53ea-a2c3-dd717c62692a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\,300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253987} {:answer/id #uuid "2d810c34-0bc0-5e74-ab6f-7603d1637eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\,300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254076} {:answer/id #uuid "42866673-7355-51c7-8216-02da859c0041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\,600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254239} {:answer/id #uuid "935d8f00-9f66-5ca5-bbc2-993f333a7dc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\,600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254962} {:answer/id #uuid "ffa1b637-25e1-5124-9fc1-639e65eef645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffa1b637-25e1-5124-9fc1-639e65eef645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255919} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1f79ec7-63e7-5ef2-a5e9-7b4a5b8637c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "061a1b6f-72e7-5f17-a2ec-c9451d7db909" :question/math-academy-id "q-121332" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1=4$  for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1 & =4 \\\\ 3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =3 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})={30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${30}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={30}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=-{180}^{∘}+{x}_{1}=-{180}^{∘}+{30}^{∘}=-{150}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-{150}^{∘},{30}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-150\\cdot 30=-4\\,500.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256549 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1eea0a4b-659c-5cbb-9b2f-823fdac924aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "346" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253935} {:answer/id #uuid "4905e33b-2819-5cbc-880c-6156d3d2ae3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "328" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254298} {:answer/id #uuid "9dff6881-5648-5a9c-a010-dec72d9bed87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "360" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255060} {:answer/id #uuid "aa8d6727-f969-5406-b3e4-f2c613ba39f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "330" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255179} {:answer/id #uuid "b57fd276-09fa-567d-9ac3-a34d3064f42d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "374" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9dff6881-5648-5a9c-a010-dec72d9bed87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "360" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255060} :answer-field/id #uuid "c68a6651-767c-5523-953b-1c67ce829142" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "90dbf916-5212-5cdb-a29f-9a2b1188a1f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-121557" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the exact solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{5x}{4})=-1$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x}{4} & =-{45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & =-{180}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘} \\\\ x & =-{36}^{∘}+n\\cdot {144}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x={108}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x={252}^{∘}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ So, the solutions are $x={108}^{∘},{252}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}$ is $108+252=360.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256563 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81234c62-eda2-52d0-b749-8f3cef95fafd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|,{5}^{5t+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254811} {:answer/id #uuid "9b4cbe0e-6e0f-5e64-aafe-1f5d3528098c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}},{5}^{5t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255038} {:answer/id #uuid "9f642cd1-e839-5656-a364-c1e30fba40f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}},{5}^{5t}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255074} {:answer/id #uuid "c4b75a67-1032-525d-a4b8-4a01a7112fca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|,{5}^{5t}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255428} {:answer/id #uuid "fd2a4b3e-cb8f-5b7e-bd06-ba95c08d46ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}},{5}^{5t+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd2a4b3e-cb8f-5b7e-bd06-ba95c08d46ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}},{5}^{5t+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255894} :answer-field/id #uuid "26052c49-cd9f-5016-b0fe-e590fff0d219" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bd538a0f-8cc3-54e7-9470-35056c79a89f" :question/math-academy-id "q-121905" :question/problem "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨\\frac{3}{t},{5}^{5t}⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{t}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({5}^{5t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}),{5}^{5t}\\cdot 5\\operatorname{ln}⁡5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}},{5}^{5t+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256566 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49f8fbca-5f34-5844-9a52-95edb85f8734" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462031} {:answer/id #uuid "a3ffa875-f647-56a7-9232-10baef0e9478" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3ffa875-f647-56a7-9232-10baef0e9478" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462032} :answer-field/id #uuid "fae7fe8f-ddd3-552e-8975-6e4558ea14d3" :answer-field/key "The point $(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462769} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ef04550-d3a4-57b1-a748-eeeb5023620d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462033} {:answer/id #uuid "394d58a3-f8ae-5072-b5e2-3fce85f31779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ef04550-d3a4-57b1-a748-eeeb5023620d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462033} :answer-field/id #uuid "928a2012-f5f2-59a3-ac6c-312a288f3f22" :answer-field/key "The point $(1,-9)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462770} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5dc12292-79a6-50bd-84c6-939caf1b310b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462035} {:answer/id #uuid "1a64fdf1-ee4e-5645-824c-0f54e90c04dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462036}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5dc12292-79a6-50bd-84c6-939caf1b310b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462035} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c38d99d-7a66-5556-8167-89059dae8fb8" :answer-field/key "The point $(3,-1)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "77063086-e28a-5987-a204-82a0d5d56c68" :question/math-academy-id "q-12195" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-4,-5),(0,2),(1,-2).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under a counterclockwise rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians about the origin?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The rotation matrix ${R}_{\\theta }$ represents a rotation of angle $\\theta $ counterclockwise about the origin and is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ into the above, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the transformation, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -5 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the rotation $R$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -5 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\sqrt{2} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\sqrt{2} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are \n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{2}}),\\,(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}),\\,(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}).\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is “The point $(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256571 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88f9e609-ec20-5a6c-849e-ce2cf1912782" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+8),t+2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254885} {:answer/id #uuid "9fc0857d-29dd-5689-a437-16c75eaea28f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=⟨\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2},2t+1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255079} {:answer/id #uuid "afc075e1-cc7d-592b-8a87-deb4ff3f3586" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5t+5),2t+1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255215} {:answer/id #uuid "c6b3c669-19ee-5d47-825e-fd4564de267b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=⟨\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2},4t-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255445} {:answer/id #uuid "d4d0a543-61f3-5785-af93-7350774e47e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5t+5),4t-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255560}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4d0a543-61f3-5785-af93-7350774e47e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5t+5),4t-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255560} :answer-field/id #uuid "d68ed885-b5cd-5bfd-8897-0ecfea6b752f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43b5d50d-4146-5a26-8f1f-16794c090f45" :question/math-academy-id "q-122056" :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨\\frac{1}{t+1},4⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡10,3⟩$ when $t=1,$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\frac{1}{t+1},4⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\frac{1}{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)+{C}_{1},4t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡10,3⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+1)+{C}_{1},4(1)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡10,3⟩ & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+{C}_{1},4+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡10 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡10-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ {C}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{10}{2}) \\\\ {C}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =4+{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{2} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡5,4t-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡[5(t+1)],4t-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5t+5),4t-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256596 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c74de54-ef15-5261-ab68-ec54271de993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254063} {:answer/id #uuid "33756f42-a85e-5e04-b840-93b27688a2d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254115} {:answer/id #uuid "3dab61a3-573e-5169-acd1-918377447d1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254204} {:answer/id #uuid "4edb2aa3-166f-5a5e-9f9a-1dc6bc4f698f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254354} {:answer/id #uuid "d5f5278b-0581-50ba-8775-861f3f25cf4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c74de54-ef15-5261-ab68-ec54271de993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254063} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fe45924-f2b0-5ada-a738-64fc16006696" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5bcc97ab-dc6e-572b-9567-59b7c4213813" :question/math-academy-id "q-122298" :question/problem "In box $A,$ there are $7$ red marbles and $3$ green marbles. In box $B,$ there are $2$ red marbles and $8$ green marbles. If we randomly select one of the boxes and then we randomly select one marble from that box, what is the probability that it’s a green marble?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let $G$ be the event that a green marble is selected, and let $A$ be the event that box $A$ is selected. Then ${A}^{′}$ is the event that box $B$ is selected. \n\nTo calculate the probability that a selected marble is green, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(G)=P(G|A)P(A)+P(G|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since there are $2$ boxes, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=\\frac{1}{2},\\,P({A}^{′})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Since there are $3$ green marbles of the total of $10$ marbles in box $A$ and $8$  green marbles of the total of $10$ marbles in box $B,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(G|A)=\\frac{3}{10},\\,P(G|{A}^{′})=\\frac{8}{10}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a selected marble is green is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(G) & =P(G|A)P(A)+P(G|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{10})(\\frac{1}{2})+(\\frac{8}{10})(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{20}+\\frac{8}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256603 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2da06fcc-39d5-5fd9-9fdf-dd52ccdbebb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254078} {:answer/id #uuid "53e9fb61-38f9-50e7-848b-f4f53f073d5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t-\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254402} {:answer/id #uuid "b006fd75-7355-56e0-986d-31463204dc8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\pi +t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255219} {:answer/id #uuid "e45f6fc8-f57d-5871-9a4c-9a83794c79ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255691} {:answer/id #uuid "f36dca5a-9486-5470-9b00-1a4d28b97e8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\pi t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e45f6fc8-f57d-5871-9a4c-9a83794c79ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255691} :answer-field/id #uuid "e344a9bb-3e71-57fd-bc54-29f1efec8291" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256324}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "74ce0e61-7285-5a7d-b7a9-5a53effb65f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-122312" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, the velocity is given by $v(x)=(1+4{x}^{2})\\,m/s.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t$ where $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{8}].$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/59/59c4a51d419b6e42633579b687cde16a2842895c589f818942c10aa304bc98a7.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)=1+4{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1+4{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1+4{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+(2x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡2x & =t+K \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t+C) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=0,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡2t.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256622 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43a37d05-93cf-5d34-976f-81987d20a896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.9\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254247} {:answer/id #uuid "53e374ac-50ea-5858-84fb-e361a9850e25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.1\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254401} {:answer/id #uuid "5ea0cc67-272f-5bd1-97cc-b3ff160fe065" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.1\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254514} {:answer/id #uuid "91f4e784-aae3-566d-9825-bc8ecd6f410b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.6\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254950} {:answer/id #uuid "b4314fca-414f-5aaa-ade9-d4f42fd6b5e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.7\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ea0cc67-272f-5bd1-97cc-b3ff160fe065" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.1\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254514} :answer-field/id #uuid "1685d0b0-562f-56c8-a207-4572c25c4216" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac224c51-f1a6-5430-b1c6-c86ec1a024d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-122348" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of decreasing $x$ where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$ If $v=-\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m},$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x=3\\,\\text{m}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "![](images/32/3278eca2ae91f59c7c20d91f9190611291924dcc0cd6ffe74456552ba8400335.png)\n\nSince the magnitude of the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1})\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=-2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=-\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(-\\sqrt{\\pi })^{2} & =-2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1+C \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{2} & =-2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}+C \\\\ C & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\pi -2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2(\\pi -2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2(\\pi -2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)<0,$ so we can discard the positive square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=-\\sqrt{2(\\pi -2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =-\\sqrt{2(\\pi -2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡3)} \\\\  & ≈-1.1\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256634 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "274fe4a8-6cce-52a7-85ec-0d6a226e2917" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254018} {:answer/id #uuid "5542a216-87ad-544f-9d6b-5abb9e3e1f21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254411} {:answer/id #uuid "578484d8-1077-5c92-bfb6-e6e025f6d837" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254438} {:answer/id #uuid "62c397c2-915b-558a-9130-f8d6eb60c818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254555} {:answer/id #uuid "8275d36a-adf0-584b-90fe-0f8e644740d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "274fe4a8-6cce-52a7-85ec-0d6a226e2917" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254018} :answer-field/id #uuid "c839859b-a531-55ec-bf20-ba653023fd33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ef0060ad-2118-50e0-9400-f7e39deea34c" :question/math-academy-id "q-122398" :question/problem "There are two bags in a box, $A$ and $B.$ In bag $A,$ there are $7$ red marbles and $3$ green marbles. We choose a bag at random with a probability of $\\frac{1}{2}$ each, and then we pick a marble from the chosen bag. The probability that the result of this experiment is a green marble is $\\frac{1}{2}.$ What is the probability that a randomly selected marble from bag $B$ is green?" :question/worked-solution "Let $G$ be the event that a green marble is selected, and let $A$ be the event that bag $A$ is selected. Then ${A}^{′}$ is the event that bag $B$ is selected. \n\nTo calculate the probability that a selected marble is green, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(G)=P(G|A)P(A)+P(G|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since there are $2$ bags, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=\\frac{1}{2},\\,P({A}^{′})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Since there are $3$ green marbles of the total of $10$ marbles in bag $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(G|A)=\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected marble in both bags is green is $\\frac{1}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nP(G)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(G|{A}^{′}),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(G) & =P(G|A)P(A)+P(G|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =(\\frac{3}{10})(\\frac{1}{2})+P(G|{A}^{′})(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{3}{20}+\\frac{1}{2}P(G|{A}^{′}) \\\\ 20\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) & =20\\cdot (\\frac{3}{20}+\\frac{1}{2}P(G|{A}^{′}))​ \\\\ 10 & =3+10P(G|{A}^{′}) \\\\ 7 & =10P(G|{A}^{′}) \\\\ P(G|{A}^{′}) & =\\frac{7}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected marble from bag $B$ is green is $\\frac{7}{10}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256639 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2101350a-8239-5482-b969-02deba016e5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-{e}^{4}}{{e}^{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253961} {:answer/id #uuid "99035da6-f3f2-54b5-b981-b2a041a9b5d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{e}^{4}-4}{{e}^{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255022} {:answer/id #uuid "9eca87f8-0f83-5f18-9369-ff1f5bab115c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255069} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb814d0-e382-5c62-a7c8-deebd241e10c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255498} {:answer/id #uuid "f1104489-4015-57f8-8ee5-881460bd6945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2+4{e}^{7}}{{e}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99035da6-f3f2-54b5-b981-b2a041a9b5d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{e}^{4}-4}{{e}^{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255022} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3f3c835-a9de-59ad-bcd7-3b129870afab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d260e2aa-2690-5911-adb6-91c0182848ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-122441" :question/problem "![](images/e2/e27774d260c053a7835b30e62c2d4b41be4b8dc22efcba8f7aa95704590561cf.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y-3}$ along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ traversed from the point $(0,-4)$ to the point $(4,0),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $4$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as  \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+16{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y-3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3}\\cdot 4\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3,$ $\\text{d}u=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3}\\,\\text{d}t & =4{\\int }_{-7}^{-3}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\,{e}^{u}{|}_{-7}^{-3} \\\\  & =4({e}^{-3}-{e}^{-7}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{e}^{3}}-\\frac{4}{{e}^{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{4}-4}{{e}^{7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256645 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ae37851-a0d1-53e2-b501-6d36f95804d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254473} {:answer/id #uuid "6660ad6b-14ab-59ff-aae0-62de46bd7c3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3-{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254591} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d5f8a9-f3a6-5e11-b9a9-f21dc78cfe93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(1-3{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255388} {:answer/id #uuid "c200c6de-7fef-560d-818d-ca3a15b06302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255403} {:answer/id #uuid "ee479383-a77b-513c-bb8f-65445e7f3153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(1-3{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255754}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee479383-a77b-513c-bb8f-65445e7f3153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(1-3{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255754} :answer-field/id #uuid "1204ced4-8121-563f-8cb8-ab8c27eb4c63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "214438e3-d9f9-5d15-b742-ace73de66292" :question/math-academy-id "q-122489" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=3-{y}^{2},$ traversed once along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $3$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=3-{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3-(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =3-9{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3-9{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot 3\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(1-3{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256648 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c8b00ce-a786-5121-9487-b06fd283c42e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254775} {:answer/id #uuid "85c6da09-ef8b-5491-a777-eb3961a3a067" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254856} {:answer/id #uuid "b2dcc70e-95cc-5391-8f38-8b2c6974aeca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255245} {:answer/id #uuid "c07d343b-43d9-5818-82d0-fbe52464d132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255382} {:answer/id #uuid "c2ad8da8-7a78-552b-8a56-6664c6283747" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c8b00ce-a786-5121-9487-b06fd283c42e" :answer/type 